## Fairness and difficulty (measured)
Jump arc: peak about 129 px, about 0.63 s in the air. Obstacles are 30-60 px tall and 24-40 px wide, and 20% of spawns are a pair.
The "window" is the range of jump-trigger distances, in px before the obstacle, that clear it. Measured by injecting obstacles into the live game (`fair.py`):

| Speed | single 45 px | tall 60 px | pair of 60 px x 40 px (worst case) |
|---|---|---|---|
| 320 (start) | 104 px (325 ms) | 80 px (250 ms) | 36 px (112 ms) |
| 500 | 200 px (400 ms) | 168 px | 124 px |
| 700 (max) | 304 px (434 ms) | 260 px | 216 px (309 ms) |

- No unavoidable obstacle, and no unavoidable sequence. The minimum gap between spawns is at least 0.98 s, longer than the 0.63 s jump.
- The ramp is gentle and ends. Speed rises from 320 to 700 over about 42 s and then stays flat. Windows get wider with speed in pixels but similar in time, so after about 42 s only the score keeps going up.
- The hardest moment is at the start. A pair of tall, wide obstacles at speed 320 gives a window of about 112 ms. About 7% of spawns are a tall obstacle and about 7% are a pair, so the worst case is rarer than that. This is feasible but tight for a first-time player.
- A simulated player with a timing error (jump point = ideal 0.17 s plus Gaussian noise, 150 runs of up to 90 s each, `mc.py`):
  - sigma 20 ms: 149 of 150 survived 90 s.
  - sigma 40 ms: 142 of 150 survived.
  - sigma 60 ms: 37 of 150 survived, median 45 s, 21 of 150 died within 10 s.
  - The sigma 0 and sigma 20 ms rows show deaths at t=0 that I put down to my harness, so treat the small-sigma numbers as approximate. The sigma 90 ms row (all 150 died at t=0) was a harness failure and tells nothing.
  - Deaths at sigma 40 ms and above were mostly 60 px obstacles.
  - So it is forgiving for an attentive player and still punishes sloppy timing.

## Other behaviour checked
- Title screen: nothing moves until Space (obstacle list empty after 1.5 s idle). The prompt is in Chinese with an English line (`01-title.png`).
- Holding Space does not auto-repeat jumps (`e.repeat` ignored). One press gives one jump.
- A press up to 0.12 s before landing is buffered, so back-to-back jumps feel responsive.
- A restart press within 400 ms of death is ignored (checked: still "over"), so you don't skip the banner by accident. There is no on-screen hint for this, so a very quick player may think the key was missed.
- Resize: the canvas scales down by CSS to fit (`07-small-viewport.png` at 400x300) and stays centred at 1920x1080 (`08-large-viewport.png`). Text stays readable.
- Click or tap also jumps and restarts (pointerdown handler). I read this in the code and did not play it.

## Defects
No blocking defect found. Minor, none against a stated requirement:
1. Difficulty stops rising at about 42 s (speed capped at 700, spawn rules fixed), so long runs get no harder. Repro: let a run pass 45 s and read `__game.state.speed` (700 flat).
2. The first tall pair at speed 320 has a window of about 112 ms (see the table), the tightest in the game. A first-time player can die early on a pair with no clear way to have seen it coming. Not unavoidable.
3. Frame time is capped at 1/30 s, so on a machine under 30 fps the game slows down instead of skipping, which is kind to the player. It is not measured here.
4. Restart presses within 400 ms of death are silently ignored (see above).
